# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
    "base": [
        ("Probing a base at more than one spot before deciding",
         "<p>Deciding whether a {city} lawn's base can stay or has to come out means probing several spots across the yard, not just the one area that looks worst from the porch. A rod or shovel pushed in at a handful of points shows whether the material underneath still springs back firm or gives with a soft, wet feel, and a base that's solid in three spots and spongy in a fourth usually needs the whole section rebuilt rather than patched around the one bad reading. Checking broadly before quoting avoids discovering a second failed area halfway through the tear-out.</p>"),
        ("A base that looks flat but was never washed to begin with",
         "<p>An older {city} lawn's base can appear level and well-compacted on the surface while still holding a fines crust from subgrade material that was never washed before the original install, years before the current standard required it. That crust doesn't announce itself as a problem until the turf above it is pulled up during a replacement, at which point it's clear the base was never actually draining the way it looked like it was. Flat isn't the same as functional, which is why we check for a crust, not just for slope, before deciding a base can stay.</p>"),
        ("Regrading a slope that drifted over years of settling",
         "<p>Florida's fine sand settles unevenly over a decade or more, and a {city} lawn that had a proper one to two percent grade on installation day can end up with a flatter slope, or even a slight reverse pitch in one corner, by the time it's due for replacement. Regrading during a tear-out is often the first real correction that slope has had since the original install, since a settling shift this gradual rarely shows up as an obvious problem until years of small changes add up to standing water somewhere new.</p>"),
        ("Working a base rebuild around plantings that stayed put",
         "<p>Mature landscaping installed after the original turf went in, a hedge line, a paver walkway added later, can box in how much of a {city} lawn's base gets fully rebuilt during a replacement versus how much gets corrected in place. A section running close to an established root system or a fixed hardscape edge sometimes gets recompacted and regraded rather than fully excavated, while an open area with nothing constraining it gets torn out completely. The base plan for a replacement has to work around what's changed on the lot since the first install, not just what the original footprint assumed.</p>"),
        ("Why a base that drained fine years ago might not now",
         "<p>Grading changes on neighboring lots, new construction nearby, or even a few extra seasons of heavy rain can shift how water moves across a {city} property compared with when the original turf went in, so a base built correctly the first time can end up sitting lower relative to water flow than it used to. Replacement is the point where that shift actually gets addressed, since raising or regrading the base to match current conditions is far more practical while the turf is already up than as a standalone project later.</p>"),
    ],
    "product": [
        ("Swapping old crumb rubber for a compliant infill",
         "<p>A {city} lawn or general play area installed years ago sometimes has crumb rubber or another synthetic infill spread across the whole yard rather than confined to equipment fall zones, which was common before the current rule limited rubber and other synthetic infill strictly to the ground under playground equipment. Replacement is the point where that infill gets swapped for clean silica sand, zeolite, or a coated sand across the open lawn area, while rubber stays only where equipment actually sits. This is one of the more common corrections we make on an older lot during a full re-turf.</p>"),
        ("Upgrading backing on a replacement instead of matching it",
         "<p>Backing technology has improved since many older {city} lawns went in, and a replacement is a natural point to move up from a semi-perforated or lower-rated backing to a fully permeable product rated above thirty inches an hour, rather than matching whatever the original install used. A homeowner replacing turf that always drained a little slower than it should doesn't have to accept the same performance a second time; the new backing is chosen for what the yard needs now, not for consistency with a product that's being pulled out anyway.</p>"),
        ("Reconsidering pile height for how the yard is used today",
         "<p>A {city} lawn installed as a general residential lawn a decade ago might now serve a household with a dog that joined the family well after the original install, which changes what pile height and infill actually fit the yard's current use. Replacement is the moment to reconsider that mismatch rather than reinstalling the same taller, looser residential blend over a yard that's really functioning as a pet run now. Matching the new product to current use, not to what was there before, avoids repeating a mismatch for another ten to twenty years.</p>"),
        ("Choosing a PFAS-free product during a replacement",
         "<p>Florida's turf standard bars intentionally added PFAS in any new synthetic turf installation, and a {city} homeowner replacing an older lawn gets to choose from current product lines built to that standard rather than whatever was on the market when the original turf went in. Asking a supplier for documentation on this point during a replacement is a reasonable ask, since it's exactly the kind of material change a full re-turf is positioned to address that a smaller repair job never would be.</p>"),
        ("Picking a better-stabilized product for a lawn that wore out early",
         "<p>Turf generally lasts ten to twenty years depending on UV stabilization, traffic, and infill, and a {city} lawn that wore out closer to the ten-year mark on a full-sun lot is a reasonable case for choosing a more UV-stable product the second time around rather than defaulting to the same specification. Spending a little more on stabilization during a replacement, when the labor and base work are already committed either way, is one of the more direct ways to push a new install toward the longer end of that lifespan range.</p>"),
    ],
    "process": [
        ("Staging demo so old debris doesn't contaminate a kept base",
         "<p>When part of a {city} lawn's base is solid enough to reuse, the old turf and infill coming out of the rest of the yard get staged and hauled away in a way that keeps loose material from falling back into the section being kept. Skipping this staging step risks mixing years-old, possibly crusted infill back into a base that was otherwise fine, undoing the reason for keeping it in the first place. This sequencing matters more on a replacement than a first-time install, since there's old material moving through the site that a new-build job never has to manage.</p>"),
        ("Sizing up the haul-off before the truck arrives",
         "<p>Old turf and infill from a full {city} yard can add up to a substantial weight once it's rolled and stacked, and estimating that volume before the tear-out starts shapes how many trips a haul-off actually takes and how the crew stages material on site while work continues. Underestimating it mid-job means a delay waiting on a second trip, which is avoidable with a rough weight and volume estimate worked out from the yard's square footage before the first section of old turf ever comes up.</p>"),
        ("Finding every irrigation head, not just the visible ones",
         "<p>An older {city} lawn's sprinkler layout isn't always fully documented, so part of a replacement's process is running water to each zone before demo starts and flagging where heads actually sit, rather than relying on memory or an old irrigation plan that may not match what's actually in the ground. A head buried under years of turf and infill is easy to miss without this step, and finding it after the new base is already compacted means reopening finished work to cap a line that should have been caught earlier.</p>"),
        ("Scheduling a rebuild around the rainy season's compaction window",
         "<p>Base material compacts properly only when it's reasonably dry, so a {city} replacement scheduled during the June-through-September rainy season needs tighter weather windows than the same job in a drier month, sometimes splitting demo and base work across a few days to catch dry stretches between afternoon storms. A replacement planned for the dry season has more flexibility, which is one reason timing a full tear-out and rebuild around the calendar, not just around a homeowner's availability, affects how smoothly the job actually goes.</p>"),
        ("A full-perimeter check before calling a replacement finished",
         "<p>A single-issue repair only needs its one seam or edge inspected before wrapping up, but a {city} replacement covering a whole yard gets a full perimeter and seam walk-through against a checklist before the crew leaves, since a full re-turf has far more linear feet of new edge and seam than any repair visit does. Catching a marginal spot during that final walk-through, while the crew and equipment are still on site, is far simpler than scheduling a separate callback for something that should have been caught before the job was called done.</p>"),
    ],
    "risks": [
        ("Old infill left mixed into a new install",
         "<p>Skipping a full infill removal during a {city} replacement, especially where old crumb rubber or another synthetic infill was spread across a general lawn area, risks leaving that material mixed into the new build rather than confined to where it's actually allowed. A new installation that still has old rubber infill scattered through it hasn't actually corrected the mismatch it was supposed to fix, and it can be hard to tell from the surface that any of the old material is still present once new turf covers it.</p>"),
        ("A base that looked fine and wasn't checked closely enough",
         "<p>Reusing a {city} lawn's base without probing it in more than one spot risks missing a fines-crusted section that looks compacted and level but still isn't draining the way new, washed material would. That kind of shortcut doesn't show up as a problem on install day, since new turf goes down over it looking exactly as it should, but the same drainage failure the old lawn had can reappear within a season or two under turf that's otherwise brand new.</p>"),
        ("An irrigation line capped with tape instead of properly sealed",
         "<p>A sprinkler line simply folded over and taped rather than cut back and capped with a proper fitting is a common shortcut on a {city} replacement, and tape doesn't hold against water pressure the way a sealed cap does. A line that fails this way months later can undermine the new base from below with a slow, hidden leak, the kind of problem that's invisible until a soft spot appears in turf that's only been down a short time. Confirming each line was properly capped, not just covered, is worth asking about directly.</p>"),
        ("Rebuilding the same footprint that never should have qualified",
         "<p>A {city} lawn's original layout sometimes sits inside a live oak's drip line, or closer than ten feet to a pond or canal without a seawall or bulkhead between them, footprints that wouldn't meet the current standard if proposed fresh today. Rebuilding to the exact same footprint during a replacement carries that same problem forward into a brand-new install rather than correcting it, when a replacement is specifically the moment a footprint like that can be adjusted without the added cost of a standalone project.</p>"),
        ("Old turf and infill that never actually leave the property",
         "<p>Disposal is part of what a replacement quote should account for, and a shortcut worth asking about directly is where the old material actually goes once it's pulled off a {city} lawn, since turf, backing, and infill have to be disposable at a permitted Florida landfill under the current standard. Material left on site, buried in a low area, or hauled informally rather than through a proper disposal process is a risk a homeowner has no easy way to verify after the fact unless it's addressed up front.</p>"),
    ],
    "care": [
        ("Watching for uneven settling through the first rainy season",
         "<p>A rebuilt or partially reused base on a {city} lawn hasn't been through a full rainy season yet right after replacement, so the first June-through-September stretch is worth watching for any low spot developing where old and new base material meet. A slight settling difference at that seam is normal and usually minor, but catching it early, while it's a small correction, is easier than waiting until the next dry season to notice a spot that's been holding water for months.</p>"),
        ("Rechecking capped valves once the season changes",
         "<p>An automatic sprinkler timer on a {city} property sometimes still has a zone programmed for an area that no longer should run, even after the head itself was properly capped during replacement, and that zone activating against a capped line can build pressure against a fitting that was never meant to hold water indefinitely. Walking through the irrigation controller after a replacement and confirming every capped zone is also turned off at the timer closes a gap that a purely physical cap doesn't address on its own.</p>"),
        ("Checking new infill depth more often in the first few months",
         "<p>Fresh infill on a {city} replacement settles and compacts faster in its first few months than infill in a mature lawn does, so checking depth against the original one to two pounds per square foot on a tighter interval right after the job, rather than waiting for the usual seasonal check, catches an area that's settled further than expected while it's still an easy top-up rather than a visibly thin patch. Traffic lanes and gate areas settle fastest, so a quick check there first usually shows whether the rest of the yard needs the same close attention or can wait for the next regular visit.</p>"),
        ("Keeping the new spec sheet for the next replacement cycle",
         "<p>The product spec sheet and invoice from a {city} replacement are worth keeping somewhere easy to find, not just for an HOA that might ask again, but for whoever handles the next replacement ten to twenty years from now, since knowing exactly what backing, infill, and base depth went in saves a round of guessing when that lawn eventually needs the same decision made again. A folder with the paperwork from this job is a small thing that saves real time on the next one.</p>"),
        ("Watching a corrected footprint for a new drainage low point",
         "<p>Moving a {city} lawn's edge outside a live oak's drip line or farther back from a setback line during replacement changes where water moves across that section of yard, even when the correction itself was overdue and necessary. The boundary where the new, adjusted footprint meets the older, unchanged part of the lawn is worth watching through the first few storms, since a grade change at that line can create a small low point that wasn't there under the old layout until the new one settles in fully.</p>"),
    ],
}
